id: pm-44u3wt
title: "Daily auto-release never runs in its declared window: all 84 scheduled runs fired 2.5-4 hours after the cron time, so the release clock is undocumented drift"
description: "Measured 2026-07-27 against the live workflow history. auto-release.yml declares schedule cron \"35 2 * * *\" and has declared exactly that value in every commit that touched the file since 2026-06-24. The GitHub API reports 84 runs for event=schedule. Not one of them started inside the declared window. The twenty most recent scheduled starts range from 05:10:04Z to 06:29:57Z, clustering near 05:35Z, which is a systematic lag of roughly three hours rather than the few minutes of jitter the platform documents. The declared cron is therefore not a description of when the project releases; it is a value nothing has ever validated against observed behaviour. Two consequences follow. First, the same-day guard computes its date key at run time, not at scheduled time, so the boundary that enforces one automatic release per day is anchored to a drifting instant that no configuration states. Second, any operator reading the workflow file forms a false expectation of the release window, and the release-health reminder that asks whether the daily release happened has no declared instant to compare against."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: "area:release",ci,observability,reliability,scheduling
created_at: "2026-07-27T04:05:01.742Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-24T13:32:41.851Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-24T13:32:40.645Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-24T13:32:40.645Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: "The observed scheduled start time is measured against the declared cron and the divergence is reported rather than assumed to be jitter; The release window is expressed as a policy the project can assert, so a run that starts outside it is a detectable condition rather than an invisible one; The same-day release guard keys off an instant whose definition is stated in the workflow and does not silently move with platform scheduling lag; The release-health verification procedure names the window it expects, so a missing release is distinguishable from a late one"
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Make the automatic release clock an asserted, observable contract instead of an unvalidated cron literal"
value: "Operators and agents can tell a late release from a failed one, and the one-release-per-day guarantee rests on a stated instant"
why_now: "The 2026-07-27 release was not produced by the schedule at all; it was driven by two manual dispatches, and the scheduled run for the day will find the work already done and skip, which reads as success"
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
environment: "GitHub Actions hosted runners, unbraind/pm-cli, workflow auto-release.yml, measured via the workflow runs API filtered to event=schedule"
repro_steps: "Run: gh api \"repos/unbraind/pm-cli/actions/workflows/auto-release.yml/runs?event=schedule&per_page=100\" --jq \".workflow_runs[].created_at\" and compare every value against the cron literal in .github/workflows/auto-release.yml"
resolution: "Added explicit nominal UTC clock, dispatch allowance, missing-run detection, separate queue timing, workflow parity test and operator documentation."
expected_result: Late and missing scheduled runs are detectable against a stated clock without changing immutable daily tag behavior.
actual_result: Focused exact-coverage and linked tests pass; live history correctly breaches the dispatch policy and the UTC execution-date tag guard remains unchanged.
