comments[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T06:14:41.159Z","harness:claude-code","Still live, verified 2026-07-31. Declared cron is \"35 2 * * *\" (02:35 UTC). Today's scheduled run created 05:50:49Z — 3h15m late. Last eleven scheduled runs all fired between 05:24:10Z and 06:05:09Z, i.e. the drift is stable at roughly three hours rather than intermittent, so the effective release clock is 05:30-06:05 UTC and no document says so."
  "2026-08-03T11:17:38.929Z","harness:codex","Current schedule evidence 2026-08-03: workflow declares cron 02:35 UTC; scheduled run 30788639874 started 05:57:42Z, a delay of about 3 hours 23 minutes, and succeeded. Functional release safety is intact, but the documented schedule-window and drift-SLO issue remains live."
  "2026-08-04T09:19:17.100Z","harness:codex","Fresh schedule evidence 2026-08-04: declared cron remains 02:35 UTC while scheduled Auto Release run 30880997668 started 05:32:20Z, about 2h57m late, and succeeded. Functional daily publication is healthy, but the declared-versus-observed release window remains materially different."
  "2026-08-15T13:57:03.009Z","harness:codex","Schedule-window evidence 2026-08-15: the workflow still declares cron 35 2 * * *, while the current scheduled run began at 03:19Z, roughly 44 minutes after the declared instant. This current sample does not match the historical 2.5-4 hour delay captured in the title, so that range must remain historical evidence rather than a universal forecast. The core gap remains: neither local nor hosted policy defines, measures, or alerts on an acceptable dispatch window. Track declared instant, observed start, queue delay, execution duration, and recovery start separately, with calendar/time-zone semantics explicit."
  "2026-09-06T07:35:30.301Z","harness:codex","Schedule evidence 2026-09-06: both local and remote main auto-release.yml declare 35 2 * * * (02:35 UTC). Today GitHub created scheduled Auto Release run 34018707399 at 07:16:32Z on 4f551f5d4a638bb0468af7d2e4d203790aae88ec, 4h41m32s after the declared instant. The workflow remains active and the run is executing the release pipeline. This is dispatch-delay evidence, not a successful-publication claim; preserve it separately from the terminal gate result and one-UTC-day tag invariant. No duplicate schedule item was created and this measurement does not claim implementation of the open policy/SLO criteria."
  "2026-09-24T13:04:15.756Z","harness:codex","Implementation paired with pm-e70zh5: compare schedule-created and runner-start timestamps separately against the 02:35 UTC nominal occurrence, declare the allowed dispatch window and missing-run deadline, and retain execution-day semantics for the existing one-release-per-day guard. Historical cron association is an inferred nearest preceding occurrence because GitHub does not expose an authoritative scheduled timestamp."
  "2026-09-24T13:14:30.260Z","harness:codex","Timing policy implementation distinguishes nominal 02:35 UTC occurrence, 60-minute dispatch allowance, queue delay, and latest overdue missing occurrence. A real 30-day GitHub census reports dispatch-window violations rather than treating historical multi-hour delays as healthy. Cron-policy parity and missing-run negative controls are included."
  "2026-09-24T13:32:39.370Z","harness:codex","Verification: policy/cron parity, late dispatch, queue separation, missing schedule and insufficient sample negative controls pass. The unchanged release guard uses the UTC execution date; the reporting clock explicitly infers the nearest preceding 02:35 UTC occurrence with a 60-minute allowance. Real historical lag remains a policy violation, not a reason to fit thresholds to failures."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-24T13:19:18.930Z","harness:codex","GitHub exposes creation and start timestamps, not an authoritative intended cron instant. Report nearest-preceding UTC occurrence as an inference and retain its greater-than-24-hour ambiguity; keep tag identity anchored to the documented execution day."
